<script setup lang="ts">
// The dead camel thorn of Deadvlei — no foliage; the whole silhouette is
// the branching. Its own component rather than markup inside HeroChat.vue
// for two reasons: the geometry below is a wall of coordinates that would
// bury the chat component, and the illustration is worth reusing (section
// dividers, empty states) the same way DuneSkyline.vue is.
//
// Drawn in DuneSkyline.vue's idiom deliberately: filled shapes that taper
// to a point rather than strokes of constant width, which is what gives
// those trees their flat, cut-paper look. Every limb is an outline sampled
// along its curve and offset by a width shrinking from base to tip, so a
// twig ends in a point and a fork joins seamlessly — and since they all
// share one fill they are subpaths of a single <path>.
//
// The shape is generated, not hand-drawn (a recursive branch walk with a
// fixed seed). Regenerating it means re-running that walk; hand-editing a
// coordinate here is not worth attempting.
//
// No props: size and place it from the outside via CSS on the element, the
// same contract DuneSkyline.vue has. The viewBox is landscape because the
// crown is wider than the tree is tall — keep width and height in that
// ratio or the tree squashes.
</script>

